A||||4||theogre||HOW TO: MAT & Coolant Sensors||||1||
Z||000000||theogre||04-02-2000||07:34 PM||||<B>WARNING! DO NOT over tighten the MAT into the 4 cylinder manifold. You can split the manifold.</B><P>The ECM Coolant and MAT sensor are 3/4 inch NPT screw in sensors. The ECM coolant and MAT sensors DO NOT use a grounded shell. Use Teflon thread tape to seal these. You can use a 3/4 inch 6 point deep socket to remove and install them. (Do Not use 12 point sockets on brass!)<P>MAT: (87-88 4 cyl, all V6)<P>The Manifold Air Temperature sensor, also called an Air Charge Sensor, uses a thermistor mounted in a cage to read intake manifold air temperature. (Used in 87-88 4 cyl.) This sensor is prone to being damaged by engine backfires.<P>If you've had engine backfires thru the intake you should remove this sensor to make sure it's not been damaged. <P>Note: I believe the V6 also uses an air charge temp sensor. It's located in the air filter can. It's installed the same way as the 4 cyl one. This one is less likely to be damaged by backfire but more suseptable to corosion. Always inspect it when changing the air filter. Make sure it's thermistor is not covered in dirt. (The thermistor is the small device inside the cage.)<P>ECM Coolant:<P>The new style coolant sensor uses a wether packed connector. The old style round connector sensor is no longer available. This means that when an old style sensor is replaced the connector will also have to be replaced. (Borg/Warner includes the new connector. Others may not.)<P>When replacing the connector YOU MUST SOLDER the connector leads to the ECM leads. The joints must also be wrapped with waterproofing. If you don't do this you will have problems in sort order. The acurate resistance of this circuit is critical to good running engines.<P>FAN, Dash warning, dash gauge coolant:<P>These sensors MUST have a good shell ground or the circuits they control will not work. DO NOT use Teflon sealing tape on these sensors. Instead use the same pipe thread sealer used to run threaded iron gas pipe. Don't wipe the excess sealer off after installing the sensor.<BR> <p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 04-09-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Cliff Pennock||04-02-2000||07:41 PM||||Ogre, what would the symptoms be of a bad MAT sensor?||||reg||5
Z||000002||Ozzy||04-02-2000||11:12 PM||||Probably "Karma" type symptoms Cliff!!  You know...from having a bunch o' forum members just about peeing their pants at the thought of free and easy horsepower!! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Ozzy ||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||04-03-2000||07:01 PM||||I wasn't quite finished, but I was tired......<P>The coolant sensor bad or getting bad connection will skew the whole fuel map. The MAT could potentially do the same thing. The MAT is used to guesstimate the air density.<P>You can test both the MAT and coolant sensors using the following numbers. They apparantly use the same NTC thermistor inside.<P>ohms-deg f/deg-c<BR>7500-40/4<BR>3400-70/20<BR>1800-100/38<BR>450-160/70<BR>185-210/100<P>You can't count entirely on the resistance tho. You also have to make sure that they are free of dirt. Dirt will insulate the sensor and make it slow.<P>The MAT must also not be touching the cage or covered in carbon. <P>Carbon will skew the resistance on the plastic caged sensors. It will insulate the metal caged ones. The plastic cage ones use an unjacketed Thermisor. These respond faster than the jacketed ones in the metal caged sensor.<P>If the sensor is slow it's not as bad as skewed. Skewed will mess with the air fuel mix. I'm guessing that my crappy looking one may have been part of my code 45's when the weather was cold. <P>If the sensor is slow you generally won't know a performance problem unless it's horribly so. You'll likely get a bit of a performance and/or milage problem untill the coolant sensor catches up with the actual water temp.<P>the Borg Warner ones I put on where less than $20us each. If the car has the old <BR>style coolant sensor I'd replace it. That round connector is known for problems.<P>On the V6 i'd test the MAT and make sure it's clean. If it's thermistor is corroded at all replace it even it it tests good.<P>On the 4 you really need to pull it and inspect it. If the thermistor has been blasted by a backfire you should replace it, especially if it's the plastic cage one.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||04-09-2000||11:34 AM||||I had to think about this a few more days to answer Cliff better.......<P>As you likely know, the MAT, ACT, or what ever GM is calling it this week,<BR>is used to measure incoming air temp.<P>If the thing is off and the ECM thinks the air temp is +-10F of reality then<BR>the ECM is going to calculate the mix wrong. A few degrees off isn't likely<BR>to cause and ECM error. What it can do is cause detonation of the mix runs a<BR>bit leaner that it should. A hair rich and it's likely just wasting gas.<P>Because of the scale of these sensors, which Looks logarithmic, a few ohms<BR>off are allot worse at some temperatures than others.<P>For example, if you are 10 ohms off at 40F it's likely not as big an effect<BR>as being 10ohms off at 100F. At 40F the 10ohm error is  0.13%, at 100F its<BR>0.55% and at 210 it's  5.40%. A 5% error is getting rather significant. (The<BR>MAT likely will never see 210F but the coolant sensor will and often.)<P>The reality is that either sensor circuit can pick up allot more than 10ohms<BR>really fast if the connectors or wiring are getting ratty. (This is why GM<BR>did away with the round connector coolant sensor) If you picked up 100ohms<BR>at 210F that's over a 50% error! While you might not get a 100ohm error<BR>25-50 ohms is pretty easy. 50 Ohms would still be over a 25% error at 210F.<P>Basically if your getting rich mix errors, or knock, or performance problems<BR>that look like vacuum leaks but you can't find a leak any place...... I<BR>would check these sensors and the wiring to them. Both are used directly in<BR>calculating F/A ratio and ignition timing. Failing to go into closed loop, or delayed closed loop,<BR>could also be the Coolant sensor.<P>I can tell you that my own car has smoothed out considerably after changing<BR>these. The MAT was toasted pretty good by backfire. A quick test of it seems<BR>to be ok, but I have to believe it wasn't accurate.<P>If you have the old style coolant sensor on any car I'd replace it. The ones<BR>from Borg Warner come with the replacement weather pack connector.<P>If the MAT in the 4 cyl has been blasted I'd replace it too. In the 6 it's<BR>in the air cleaner so it's hard to blast that one. You do have to make sure<BR>the V6's MAT is clean.<P>If you're cleaning the MAT on the V6 DON'T use carb cleaner. Use an<BR>electronics cleaner the DOESN'T have lubricants in it. (NO "tuner wash")<BR>Lubricants left by a cleaner can draw dirt and moisture. You should mostly<BR>only have to dust the V6 MAT off. If anything is stuck to it then figure out<BR>why so it doesn't happen again.<P>If you're spray cleaning the 87-88 4 cyl TBI on the car, you know the shoot<BR>it on the run routine, use only a fuel injection cleaner. These should be<BR>made so as not to ruin the MAT which is in the intake to the right of the<BR>TBI. Even then don't use more than you have to and avoid stalling the car<BR>with the cleaner. If the car stalls you'll likely backfire and that can ruin<BR>the MAT.||||reg||1
